WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:07.080
How do other languages accommodate non-binary people?

00:00:08.240 --> 00:00:10.480
In the English-speaking world they and them...

00:00:10.480 --> 00:00:13.360
...are by far the most common pronouns for non-binary people...

00:00:13.360 --> 00:00:16.200
...but other languages don’t all have it quite so easy

00:00:16.760 --> 00:00:20.120
Around a third of the world’s languages use grammatical gender...

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:24.240
...in which nouns are typically sorted into male and female...

00:00:24.240 --> 00:00:27.200
...or masculine and feminine. For example...

00:00:27.200 --> 00:00:30.120
...in French "la table"—the table—is feminine...

00:00:30.120 --> 00:00:33.280
...and in German "der schlüssel"—the key—is masculine

00:00:34.480 --> 00:00:36.920
But languages with grammatical gender pose a problem...

00:00:36.920 --> 00:00:39.760
...for those who don’t identify as male or female...

00:00:39.760 --> 00:00:42.720
...so they are coming up with some innovative solutions

00:00:44.160 --> 00:00:46.120
Spanish is one language where activists...

00:00:46.120 --> 00:00:48.000
...have come up with a fairly elegant solution

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:51.080
The use of the ending -e on nouns and adjectives...

00:00:51.080 --> 00:00:55.320
...rather than the usual -o for masculine and -a for feminine

00:00:56.680 --> 00:00:59.200
And the pronoun “elle” has been invented...

00:00:59.200 --> 00:01:01.920
... to be the equivalent of Spanish’s they or them...

00:01:01.920 --> 00:01:02.960
...for non-binary people...

00:01:02.960 --> 00:01:06.240
...because it closely resembles both “él” for he...

00:01:06.240 --> 00:01:08.120
 ...and “ella” for she

00:01:08.400 --> 00:01:09.360
And in Swedish...

00:01:09.360 --> 00:01:11.400
...the pronoun “hen&lt;i&gt;” &lt;/i&gt;is gaining ground...

00:01:11.400 --> 00:01:13.480
...as a neat compromise in pronunciation...

00:01:13.480 --> 00:01:17.080
...between "han" for he, and "hon" for she

00:01:17.640 --> 00:01:19.840
But some languages are easier to tweak...

00:01:19.840 --> 00:01:22.240
...to accommodate non-binary people than others

00:01:22.600 --> 00:01:25.840
Mandarin has just one word for both he and she...

00:01:25.840 --> 00:01:28.520
...which is “tā”

00:01:28.520 --> 00:01:30.000
So far so good, right?

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:33.880
Not exactly, because in writing they have different characters

00:01:35.000 --> 00:01:38.560
Some non-binary people have begun using the Roman letter “X”...

00:01:38.560 --> 00:01:43.200
...joined with the Chinese character, or even the Roman letters “TA”

00:01:43.440 --> 00:01:45.160
But there is little consensus...

00:01:45.160 --> 00:01:48.160
...and many feel that relying on the Roman alphabet...

00:01:48.160 --> 00:01:50.200
...is sub-optimal and unnatural

00:01:50.200 --> 00:01:52.720
And in other countries non-binary people don’t...

00:01:52.720 --> 00:01:53.960
...have it so easy either

00:01:54.840 --> 00:01:55.880
French and German, for example...

00:01:55.880 --> 00:01:58.920
...like Spanish, have grammatical gender absolutely everywhere...

00:01:58.920 --> 00:02:02.160
...but they don’t have such easily available fixes

00:02:02.160 --> 00:02:04.160
It may be some time before there are...

00:02:04.160 --> 00:02:06.160
...elegant and useable solutions...

00:02:06.160 --> 00:02:08.280
...everywhere where non-binary people...

00:02:08.280 --> 00:02:11.520
 ...are seeking greater grammatical visibility

